# Wrangler-runtime secrets for `wrangler dev` (used by `npm run preview` and
# `npm run smoke`). Copy to .dev.vars (gitignored) and fill in real values.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ALONGSIDE mise.local.toml
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are different things and do not overlap:
#
#   mise.local.toml  →  sets shell env vars     →  Astro reads at BUILD time
#                                                  (PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY)
#
#   .dev.vars        →  wrangler reads at start →  Worker reads at RUNTIME
#                                                  via locals.runtime.env.*
#                                                  (BUTTONDOWN_API_KEY,
#                                                   TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY)
#
# Each variable lives in exactly one file. Worker-runtime secrets are kept
# out of shell env on purpose — only wrangler reads .dev.vars, so they
# can't leak into other tools' process.env.
#
# In PRODUCTION, .dev.vars is not used at all. Real values are set with:
#   wrangler secret put BUTTONDOWN_API_KEY
#   wrangler secret put TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY
#
# WHAT WORKS WITH THE DEFAULTS BELOW
# ───────────────────────────────────
# The defaults give you a working form for everything EXCEPT the actual
# Buttondown call: form renders → Turnstile renders + always passes (test
# secret) → /api/subscribe verifies token → Buttondown call returns 401
# (the placeholder API key isn't real) → endpoint returns 502 to client.
# That's enough to exercise the entire pipeline locally.
#
# To test the full happy path locally, replace BUTTONDOWN_API_KEY with a
# real key from a free Buttondown account (Settings → Programming → API).
#
# Turnstile test keys (always pass / never block) are documented at:
#   https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/troubleshooting/testing/

BUTTONDOWN_API_KEY=your-buttondown-api-key
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
